A||N||13||cadero2dmax||Grassroots Motorsports $2002 Challenge||||1||
Z||000000||cadero2dmax||10-16-2001||09:46 PM||||In case some of you are not aware, I am building an entry into the Grassroots Motorsports Magazine $2002 Challenge to take place in Orlando Florida next February.<P>The entry will be a Fiero, an 84 coupe with an '88 cradle and a carborated 4.9 Caddy in front of a TH125.<P>This challenge is sponsored by the magazine, and the winning entries get lots of press (along with full cover shots). When was the last time you saw a Fiero on the cover of ANY magazine?<P>Here's what it is all about. This is a 3 tiered competition, a drag race, an autocross, and "concourse" judging. The cost of building of the competing cars (including the original price of the car) cannot exceed $2002. The complete rules can be read at <A HREF="http://www.grassrootsmotorsports.com." TARGET=_blank>www.grassrootsmotorsports.com.</A> <P>I am getting the car from ED Parks, and have swapped the original engine and 4 speed for the cradle adapters and TH125. The cradle is out of an '88 coupe that burned, I will have to sand and paint it. <P>I have been purchasing "garage-sale" priced items from the Fiero nuts on the Fiero Racing List. I have acquired a set of adjustable perch sleeves and Gabriel struts for the '88 adaption, 5 point harness, a TH- 125 shift kit, swaybar poly bushings, and a NEW set of R-1 tires. Oh, and a couple of stripped 4.9 engines (kinda like a used long block). <P>There are still lots of things I need to do this project, I am looking for used parts as cheap as possible (but no donations). I need an '88 rear swaybar (preferably a larger aftermarket bar), a larger early chassis front swaybar, an aluminum or fiberglass racing seat, delrin or poly bushings for the '88 rear links, delrin or poly bushings for the front suspension, etc. Anything I can save money on will only allow me to field a more competitive car.<P>Can any on this list help?<P>G <P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Slammed Fiero||10-16-2001||09:49 PM||||Welcome to the forum George.<P>Happy hunting.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Jonathan McCreery||||reg||1
Z||000002||Nashco||10-16-2001||11:31 PM||||I've been following this on the email list, glad to see ya on the forum...it's an awesome resource.<P>So you're using the 84 front suspension and 88 rear, correct?  I have a set of 88 upper control arms with poly, but they're different than yours.  Just thought I'd check and make sure you haven't somehow swapped the 88 front too...<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||bHooper||10-17-2001||09:59 AM||||George,<P>You can get the poly bushings through NOPI.  They order them direct from prothane's parent company.  Not as quick on delivery, but definatly cheaper!<P>Hear are the part numbers:<P>7-304 is the Rear Control Arm kit, $18.87<BR>7-502 Cradle mount's, $32.37<BR>7-207 Front Control Arm kit, $32.37<P>You can't beat these prices, all for less than $100!<P>Somewhere there is a thread with ALL the parts and prices that I used in rebuilding my suspension last year.<P>So, how are you going to do all of this work for less than $2002?  The caddy engine isn't cheap... even used from a salvage yard is about $800.<P>just curious.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B>Red</B></FONT></FONT> 86 GT 5 speed<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><B>Black</B></FONT> <u>86 SE Automatic</u>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Hartz||10-17-2001||10:34 AM||||I've followed the previous GRM challege series, and always thought about how you might do a Fiero.  Do you have a high level plan you could share with us?  I'd be really interested.<P>Best of luck.<P>Hartz<P>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo<BR>hartzfeld.home.mindspring.com||||reg||1
