Z||000006||Philphine||10-17-2001||11:51 AM||||how nice dose the car have to be for the concourse part? i know weight is an important factor, but do you keep any interior beyond whats required? do creature comforts count? is it a full third of the score or just a small amount? i guess i want to know if after the challenge would you have something you could or would want to drive later. as a street car.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Philphine||10-17-2001||12:15 PM||||sorry to keep asking stuff. it's really interesting to me. do you have a cost breakdown for where you are so far? is labor figured in? do you have a page with more details or an addy to this list with more info?||||reg||1
Z||000008||cadero2dmax||10-20-2001||10:48 AM||||A lot of questions were posted, I will try to answer (and be brief).<P>The Grassroots Motorsport project will be chronicled on a mailing list at: <BR>GrassrootsChallengeFiero@yahoolists.com<P>Included will be some photos, and a spreadsheet showing all costs incurred.<P>I intend to attack the drag part with the 300lbft 4.9 that puts it's torque down immediately. This motor doesn't have the horsepower of some of the other V8's, but the torque comes on sooner, peaking around 2400RPM. This engine pulls like a diesel (and is RPM limited, as a result). I intend to put the power to the ground through a 2.86:1 ratio TH125 3 speed with a 2400 stall convertor and shift kit. I chose the automatic because it bolts right up, and no flywheel needs built (costly). Remember, I am on a strict budget!<P>The engine will be carburated, to make the conversion less costly also. I am buying engines from a builder that strips them of the injectors, sensors, wiring, etc. for a 4.9 swap. He uses new longblocks, I simply and essentially buy the used longblock from him for a song.<P>The autocross portion will be attacked by doing the suspension basically as I did the Q4 initially. Those that know me know I can make a Fiero handle (have been racing them for years and years).<P>The concourse judging? I am not sure, but I think it is just points awarded for workmanship - but of course appearance is judged also. But since stripping the interior is allowed, for example, I don't hink a Mr. Mike's would get more points than mine with an aluminum racing seat and painted interior.<P>Other prizes - Most Spectacular Failure (blowing an engine on the strip, etc.), Long Distance Award - the Challenge car driven the furthest (I will trailer mine), etc. I do not plan on winning any of these :-)  <P>Hope this answers most questions.<P>Thanks for asking<P>G||||reg||10
Z||000009||cadero2dmax||10-20-2001||11:07 AM||||More answers to questions posed.<P>The car is an 85 coupe, with an '88 cradle/rear suspension. I swapped the stock engine and tranny for the cradle adapters, and the low gear ratio TH125 trans. I bought a "coil-over" sleeve set, and some Gabriel shocks from a friend on the FieroRacingList.<P>The cradle came from an '88 coupe that burned, so the cradle requires stripping and painting.<P>The front suspension is the stock early chassis. I will sharpen it up with poly bushings, lowering springs, and upgraded shocks (almost had some KYB's on e-Bay, but got outbid at the last minute). I will be using R-1's for autocross, and drag tires for that event.<P>The engine cost was mentioned. The 4.9 is not the N* (the Northstar is 4.6 liters anyway), it is a 16 valve 300 cubic inch aluminum engine with cast heads. It is rated at around 200hp and 300lb torque. This engine was in Cads in the early 90's. An entire wrecked Cad can be had for $500 to $1000. These engines are good for at least a couple hundred thousand miles, lightweight, and a relatively easy swap. That is why I chose it for the Challenge car.<P>Now, I think I have answered all the questions asked.<P>G<p>[This message has been edited by cadero2dmax (edited 10-21-2001).]||||reg||12
Z||000010||Slammed Fiero||10-20-2001||12:11 PM||||260hp?? i thought they were 215hp???<P>I know The pylon python uses an allante manifold setup..is this where your getting the extra power?<P>JM||||reg||1
Z||000011||cadero2dmax||10-20-2001||02:37 PM||||That was a typo, it was supposed to read around 200 hp, not 260. Actually, when I saw your reply I looked this engine up in my Chilton's, and it shows 210 hp, 300lbft torque on the specifications page. <P>I can't tell you what the carb does for power, nor can I tell you how much is gained with the 5 degree cam timing mod, or ignition timing advance with the Olds distributor.<P>This is a lightweight torque motor, I equate it's performance to my Dodge Cummins diesel, and it performs similarly. A lot of pull, limited RPM.<P>But let me tell you, I like those that rely on big numbers - - for example my Q4 racer was dyno rated at 238hp. We "de-tuned" the Q4 engine from 250+ in order to get torque above 200, and it ended up being a more tractable engine with the lesser horsepower.<P>It is torque that wins on the track, at the autocross and Pro Solo. At the strip you need a combo of both.  <P>But those that rely on big numbers are fun - the 238hp Q4 was in the 13's(in road racing trim and on road racing tires), and it truly surprised more than a few 300hp Mustangs and Camaros!!<P>Anyway, the proof is in the pudding. Let me finish this car, and we will see what that "little" motor can do in the Challenge.<P>G||||reg||1
